      <div class="article-tag">Guide</div>
      <h1>US Pay Transparency Laws by State (2026): Salary Range Rights & Negotiation Guide</h1>
      <div class="article-meta"><span>Sep 10, 2026</span><span>·</span><span>6 min read</span></div>
      <p class="article-intro">A wave of salary transparency laws across the United States has permanently changed the job application process. Employers in numerous states are now legally mandated to disclose compensation ranges in job postings. Here is what you need to know and how to use it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="container" style="max-width:740px;">
    <div class="prose">
      <div class="callout"><strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> If you apply for a job located in or advertised to workers in states with pay transparency laws (such as California, New York, Colorado, Washington, or Illinois), employers are legally required to provide a good-faith salary range.</div>

      <h2>Why Pay Transparency Laws Matter for Workers</h2>
      <p>For decades, the "salary negotiation dance" forced applicants to name a target number first, frequently underpricing their market value or wasting weeks interviewing for positions that paid below their requirements. Pay transparency legislation levels the playing field by:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Eliminating information asymmetry between job applicants and hiring managers.</li>
        <li>Combating gender and racial wage disparities for identical job classifications.</li>
        <li>Giving current employees benchmark data to request internal market adjustments.</li>
        <li>Protecting employees from retaliation when discussing compensation with coworkers.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>State-by-State Pay Transparency Law Summary</h2>
      <p>Below is a breakdown of major US states with active salary disclosure requirements:</p>
      <table>
        <tr><th>State</th><th>Employer Size Threshold</th><th>What Must Be Disclosed</th><th>Remote Job Coverage</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>California (SB 1162)</strong></td><td>15+ employees</td><td>Pay scale on all postings; available upon request to current employees</td><td>Yes, if job can be performed in CA</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>New York (State & NYC)</strong></td><td>4+ employees</td><td>Good-faith minimum and maximum salary or hourly rate on postings</td><td>Yes, if role reports to a NY office</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Colorado (Equal Pay Act)</strong></td><td>1+ employees</td><td>Hourly rate or salary range, plus general description of bonuses and benefits</td><td>Yes, any posting open to CO applicants</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Washington (SB 5761)</strong></td><td>15+ employees</td><td>Wage scale or salary range, plus general description of benefits and comp</td><td>Yes, if work can be done remotely in WA</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Illinois (HB 3129)</strong></td><td>15+ employees</td><td>Pay scale and benefits overview on external and internal postings</td><td>Yes, if reporting to an IL supervisor</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Maryland</strong></td><td>1+ employees</td><td>Wage range in all job postings; wage history inquiry ban</td><td>Yes, for positions physically or remotely in MD</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Hawaii (SB 1057)</strong></td><td>50+ employees</td><td>Hourly rate or salary range reflecting reasonable expectations</td><td>Yes, for roles based or performed in HI</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Minnesota</strong></td><td>30+ employees</td><td>Starting salary range or fixed rate plus benefit summary</td><td>Yes, if posting accessible to MN workers</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>How to Handle Suspiciously Wide Salary Ranges</h2>
      <p>Some companies post exaggerated bands (e.g., "$70,000 – $210,000") to comply with the letter of the law while obscuring actual compensation. If you encounter an excessively wide range:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Ask for Leveling Criteria:</strong> Inquire during the first interview: <em>"Could you share what differentiates a candidate at the lower end of this band versus someone at the upper quartile?"</em></li>
        <li><strong>Anchor in the Top 20%:</strong> If you possess all required qualifications and several preferred qualifications, state: <em>"Given my track record in [key skill] and industry background, my expectations align with the $160,000–$180,000 tier of your posted range."</em></li>
        <li><strong>Factor in Location Tiers:</strong> Multi-state companies often scale pay bands based on local cost of living. A remote worker in Texas or Florida may receive an offer anchored differently than a worker in San Francisco or Manhattan.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Banning the Salary History Question</h2>
      <p>Over 20 states and local jurisdictions now prohibit employers from asking about your previous salary history. Employers cannot ask: <em>"What are you currently earning?"</em> or base an offer on past pay stubs. If an interviewer asks, you can politely redirect: <em>"I am focused on the market rate for this specific responsibility set, which aligns with your posted range of $95,000 to $110,000."</em></p>

